<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harthacnut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1018–<a href="/years/1042">1042</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Hardicanute</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harthacnut was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> from 1035 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from 1040 to 1042, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emma-of-normandy">Emma of Normandy</a>. Kept in Denmark by the threat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-the-good">Magnus the Good</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, he lost England for a time to his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-harefoot">Harold Harefoot</a> but took the throne after Harold's death. His heavy taxes made him unpopular, and his sudden death at a wedding feast in 1042 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> to the English throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harthacnut.jpg" alt="Harthacnut" width="320" height="293" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harthacnut_-_MS_Royal_14_B_VI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Harthacnut was born shortly after the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emma-of-normandy">Emma of Normandy</a> in 1017. Cnut had set aside his first wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelfgifu-of-northampton">Ælfgifu of Northampton</a>, and according to the Encomium Emmae Reginae, which Emma later commissioned, agreed that sons of the new marriage would take precedence. In 1026, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> was threatened from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, Cnut sent his eight-year-old son there under a council headed by Earl Ulf. In 1027 Cnut arrived with a fleet, forgave his son's insubordination, but had Ulf murdered. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-the-good">Magnus I of Norway</a> invaded Norway in 1035, Harthacnut's half-brother Svein fled to his court, and they learned that their father had died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Harthacnut succeeded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1035 but could not come to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> because his position at home was under threat. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">Magnates</a> north of the Thames backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-harefoot">Harold Harefoot</a>, Cnut's son by Ælfgifu, who acted as regent, while Emma held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>. In late 1037 Harold was generally accepted as king and Emma went into exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>. Harthacnut and Magnus agreed that if either died without an heir his kingdom would pass to the other.</p><p>Harold died on 17 March 1040 and envoys offered Harthacnut the throne. He landed at Sandwich on 17 June with 62 warships and levied a geld of over 21,000 pounds, which made him unpopular. At Emma's urging he had Harold's body dug up and beheaded. He doubled the English fleet to thirty-two ships and raised taxes sharply during a poor harvest. In 1041 two tax collectors were killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a>, and he ordered the town burned, though most people had fled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1041 Harthacnut invited his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> back from exile in Normandy; some historians see Edward as heir, since Harthacnut had neither wife nor children and was said to suffer from frequent illness. On 8 June 1042 he attended a wedding in Lambeth where, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, he died as he stood at his drink, falling to the ground in a convulsion.</p><p>Medieval sources are mostly hostile, but some modern historians note that he kept peace across his lands. Magnus claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> after his death, a claim later pursued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a>, but Edward held the throne. Harthacnut was the last Dane to rule England.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harthacnut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Edward the Confessor, son of Æthelred the Unready and Emma of Normandy, was crowned king of the English in Winchester Cathedral, having succeeded his half-brother <b>Harthacnut</b> in 1042.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-3">3 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Edward the Confessor, son of Æthelred the Unready and Emma of Normandy, succeeded his half-brother <b>Harthacnut</b> as king of the English.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-8">8 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He governed Denmark and Norway through regents, including his son <b>Harthacnut</b> and the earls of Lade.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-sea-empire">North Sea Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kingdom of England. Died in Lambeth, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.49,-0.12&amp;year=1033" aria-label="Lambeth on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Harthacnut"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.49,-0.12&amp;year=1033">What was here in 1033? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
